{"name":"Cerebellum Grey","hex":"#cccbcd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cerebellum","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Part of the hindbrain in vertebrates. In humans it lies between the brainstem and the back of the cerebrum and is formed of two lateral lobes and a median lobe. It plays an important role in sensory perception, motor output, balance and posture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cerebellum"},{"term":"grey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray.","Synonym of coloured (pertaining to the mixed race of black and white)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cerebellum","blocks":[{"text":"The cerebellum is a major feature of the hindbrain of all vertebrates. Although usually smaller than the cerebrum, in some animals such as the mormyrid fishes it may be as large as it or even larger. In humans, the cerebellum plays an important role in motor control and cognitive functions such as attention and language as well as emotional control such as regulating fear and pleasure responses, but its movement-related functions are the most solidly established. The human cerebellum does not initiate movement, but contributes to motor coordination, precision, and accurate timing: it receives input from sensory systems of the spinal cord and from other parts of the brain, and integrates these inputs to fine-tune motor activity. Cerebellar damage produces disorders in fine movement, equilibrium, posture, and motor learning in humans."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Gehirn%2C_lateral_-_Lobi_%2B_Stammhirn_%2B_Cerebellum_eng.svg/330px-Gehirn%2C_lateral_-_Lobi_%2B_Stammhirn_%2B_Cerebellum_eng.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebellum","wikidata":"Q130983"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey","wikidata":"Q42519"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Cerebellum.gif/500px-Cerebellum.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Cerebellum.gif/500px-Cerebellum.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACerebellum.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Sobo_1909_653.png/500px-Sobo_1909_653.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Sobo_1909_653.png/500px-Sobo_1909_653.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"View of the cerebellum from above and behind","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASobo_1909_653.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/CerebellumDiv.png/500px-CerebellumDiv.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/CerebellumDiv.png/500px-CerebellumDiv.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schematic representation of the major anatomical subdivisions of the cerebellum. Superior view of an \"unrolled\" cerebellum, placing the vermis in one plane.","link":"https://en.wikipedia